{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Before labeling arbitrage “failed,” the board should establish what the closing premium/discount actually measures. Compare the ETF’s closing price with a synchronized, executable estimate of portfolio value—not a stale NAV or indicative value built from last trades. Check whether foreign securities, bonds, or other holdings were closed or thinly traded while the ETF remained open, and account for fair-value adjustments, time zones, and closing-auction effects.\n\nReview the pattern intraday and at different market closes, and compare it with peers using similar benchmarks, holdings, and trading hours. Examine spreads, depth, volatility, creation/redemption costs, settlement or transfer constraints, and whether underlying securities are hard or costly to trade. Ask APs and market makers about quoting, inventory, hedging, and creation/redemption activity, including whether participation has declined or become concentrated.\n\nSeek independent data and interviews before drawing conclusions. If warranted, consider clearer disclosures, improved valuation or trading data, outreach to liquidity providers, or operational changes. Use context-sensitive monitoring—not a single numerical trigger."}}
